= Change Log =

* 4.0.0.ah1 - AirHelp fork (major, breaking)

  The 'migration' check is rewritten and its old, silently-degrading behaviour
  is removed. This is a breaking change on purpose.

  What was wrong

    The previous implementation asked ActiveRecord whether the running app
    still had any migrations left to apply, using ActiveRecord::Migration
    .check_pending!. If ActiveRecord did not respond to that method, the gem
    silently switched to a very different behaviour: it compared the highest
    migration timestamp found on disk (the app's own db/migrate directory)
    to the highest version recorded in the schema_migrations table, and
    reported the pod as unhealthy whenever the two numbers did not match.

    That fallback answers a different question. It complains not only when
    the app is behind the database, but also when the database is ahead of
    the app. In a rolling deployment on Kubernetes those two situations are
    fundamentally different:

      * "App ahead of DB" means the new code was rolled out without its
        migrations having been applied. That is a real problem: the app
        expects columns or tables that do not exist yet, and requests will
        genuinely fail.

      * "DB ahead of app" is the normal, expected state that every
        old pod finds itself in during a rolling deployment. The moment the
        migration Job or init container of the new release finishes, every
        still-running old pod sees a database that already contains the new
        migration's version stamp - even though the old pod's image, and
        therefore its bundled migration files, obviously does not include
        that file. Those pods can continue to serve traffic correctly:
        their expected schema is still present, additional columns or
        removed indexes do not break their queries. They just happen to be
        temporarily "behind" the database until they are drained.

    Under Rails 7.1 the check_pending! entry point was deprecated in favour
    of check_all_pending!. Under Rails 7.2 check_pending! was removed
    entirely. On those versions the gem quietly fell into the fallback
    branch, meaning every release that included a database migration
    caused every old pod to fail its health check the moment the migration
    Job wrote to schema_migrations - even though nothing had gone wrong
    with those pods. Whether that translated into an outage depended on
    how sensitive readiness probes, load balancers and paging thresholds
    were: some deployments absorbed the flap, others tipped over.

  What this version does

    The migration check now uses ActiveRecord::Migration.check_all_pending!
    when available (Rails 7.1 and above), which correctly answers only the
    "app ahead of DB" question and treats "DB ahead of app" as healthy,
    which is what a rolling deployment needs.

    For Rails 4.0 through 7.0 the check falls back to the legacy
    check_pending! entry point, which has the same directional semantic.

    If neither entry point is available - which in practice means the app
    has removed or replaced ActiveRecord, or is on an unsupported ancient
    Rails - the check now raises HealthCheck::UnsupportedActiveRecordError
    instead of silently reverting to the old bidirectional comparison.
    The endpoint therefore fails loudly and explicitly, so a misconfigured
    or unsupported environment cannot masquerade as healthy.

  Breaking changes

    * The filename-vs-schema_migrations comparison is removed entirely.
      Applications that were relying on it - typically because they were
      running on pre-Rails-4 or had removed ActiveRecord - will now see
      the endpoint raise HealthCheck::UnsupportedActiveRecordError instead
      of the old "Current database version (...) does not match latest
      migration (...)" message.

    * A new HealthCheck::UnsupportedActiveRecordError exception is
      introduced. It is raised by the migration check when no supported
      entry point is exposed by ActiveRecord::Migration. Callers that
      wrap the check manually may want to rescue it.

    * The migration branch of HealthCheck::Utils.process_checks now
      delegates to HealthCheck::Utils.check_migrations, a new public
      class method that can be called directly and unit-tested in
      isolation. Its return contract is: nil on success, the pending
      migration error message on a legitimate "app ahead of DB" failure,
      raises HealthCheck::UnsupportedActiveRecordError otherwise.

  Tests

    Unit tests for HealthCheck::Utils.check_migrations are added under
    test/unit/. They cover: preferring check_all_pending! when both are
    available, falling back to check_pending! when only it is available,
    surfacing PendingMigrationError messages verbatim from either entry
    point, raising when neither entry point is exposed, and raising when
    ActiveRecord::Migration is not defined at all. A new rake task
    'test:unit' runs them without the existing bash-based Rails
    integration harness, which continues to be invoked by the default
    'test' task.

* 3.1.0 - 26 May 2021
  * Updated README to clarify railsN branch status (they are intended to be stable, development to be a feature branch off them or master)
  * Updated README with all the settings
  * Updated all the branches to work with the latest travis and gem changes, as some gems needed to be locked down.
  * Updated to test rails 6.0 and 6.1
  * Got all travis tests passing
  * Removed old, unused branches
  * Merged PR Fix broken Cache-Control headers #93 from felixbuenemann 
  * Merged PR S3 should not depend on Rails secrets file #77 by natefaerber
  * Merged PR RabbitMQ Health check #98 from rhuanbarreto
  * Merged PR Use remote_ip to accept proxy-forwarded requests #102 by alessio-signorini but made it optional
  * Fixed up failure setting to match previous output on error, and use it as a prefix when the error message is also output (not by default)
  * Always log the error to rails log even if not including in html response
